  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A succession crisis at the Beacon Stack, the Aurora Stack's primary aurora-channeling site, triggered by the death of elder mystic Veil☽ Glow without a named successor. Created during the Frozen Peace, the crisis deadlocked Aurora governance for six weeks, fractured the Radiant Aurora-harp — a ceremonial artifact that had survived the Orca Incursion — and nearly collapsed the Flipper Accord, the trade agreement sustaining both penguin civilizations. The crisis is formally catalogued as triggered by the Holdborn (ice-thresh) faction, though blame distributes more widely than any single faction deserves.
    
    ## Precipitating Event
    
    Veil☽ Glow's body lay in the aurora-chamber for three days before the council agreed she was dead. She had channeled sky-light so long that her feathers held permanent iridescence; younger mystics kept checking her breathing. The council had let her continue channeling until it killed her — a fact the official accounts tend to pass over quickly.
    
    ## Factional Deadlock
    
    Three competing claims emerged, each grounded in a different legal framework:
    
    | Faction | Claim | Legal Basis |
    |---|---|---|
    | Tide-speakers | Succession by bloodline | Argued Veil☽ Glow's intent |
    | Gift-keepers | Council vote | Gift-custom and traditional precedent |
    | Long-dark Edict adherents | Veil☽ Glow held no transferable authority | Fire-trade compromise had invalidated her station |
    
    The Dawn Mandate — the emergency measure stripping voting rights from penguins who cannot manifest aurora-light — complicated every proposed resolution, as did the Thaw-Kept provisions governing resource distribution. What might have remained ceremonial dispute became existential when the Nightshelf demanded aurora-crystals for the winter forges immediately. The recharging adepts could not agree among themselves who held authorization. Crystal stockpiles sat untouched.
    
    For six weeks, penguins from the same family-constellation refused to speak at memorial fires. Someone — accounts differ, and the ice is unhelpful — began hoarding crystal shipments in violation of Gift-custom.
    
    ## The Fracturing
    
    Ice-Crowned Shexuwohua, acting for the Holdborn faction, seized the Radiant Aurora-harp during a heated council session and split a new fracture line from an existing war-scar to the instrument's base. Multiple delegations witnessed the act. No one formally claimed responsibility. That night, four penguins Walked — stood from the memorial fires and moved toward the ice-edge with slow certainty. Their tracks led outward and did not return.
    
    The Pinnacle faction claimed custody of the fractured harp before the session adjourned, securing effective control over recharging authority — a position they retain.
    
    ## Resolution
    
    The crisis ended through exhaustion, not consensus. A compromise leader was chosen for her willingness to end the argument rather than her fitness to lead. Crystal shipments resumed at sixty percent capacity. The Nightshelf received less than promised. Noctisum~ the merchant pursued undisclosed negotiations in the margins. Xi the smuggler shifted allegiances between factions.
    
    The underlying disputes remain unresolved. The Pinnacle holds the harp. The Dawn Mandate and Gift-custom remain in tension. The Nightshelf miners cite this crisis when explaining why they do not trust the surface — and the casualty figures, such as they are, consist of four names no one recorded walking into the dark.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771581006108_0", anchor: "deadlocked Aurora governance for six weeks", text: "Thirty-nine days by the ice-record. Not six weeks. Someone rounded, and it stuck, and now every subsequent chronicle repeats it. This is how imprecision becomes canon.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771581006108_1", anchor: "split a new fracture line from an existing war-scar to the instrument's base", text: "This is the crack. The sealed but unhealed crack I have been denied permission to examine. Shexuwohua did not break the harp — she extended a wound the orca had already made during the Incursion. The Pinnacle knows what that original scar records. Their silence on my access request is not administrative delay. It is policy.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771581006108_2", anchor: "Noctisum~ the merchant pursued undisclosed negotiations in the margins", text: "Administrative ice at stratum 6 preserves three complete draft revisions of the Flipper Accord, none matching the public version. At least one dates to this crisis — the thermal signature is consistent with the deadlock period. The Accord that resumed crystal shipments was not the Accord that had governed them before. \"Undisclosed negotiations\" is my own cowardice. I can document this. The drafts are there. I should have put it in the main text and I did not, because the Council commissioned a retrospective, not an autopsy.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771581006108_3", anchor: "four names no one recorded walking into the dark", text: "Four. During thirty-nine days of fractured families, frozen crystal shipments, and a shattered sacred artifact. The deep ice beneath the Beacon Stack carries grief-residue dense enough to discolor the strata. That is not four penguins' worth of grief. That is not even close.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
